+# CSV import of contacts
+
+Status: ready-for-agent
+
+## Problem Statement
+
+Sales keeps leads in spreadsheets. To get them into contactbook today, someone has to add each contact by hand, one at a time. That is slow and error-prone for sheets with hundreds of rows. When something is wrong with a row, nobody finds out until a person notices the contact is missing or incomplete.
+
+## Solution
+
+An **import**: hand contactbook a whole CSV file (the same columns as our CSV export) and get back an **import report**. The report says which contacts were **created**, which existing contacts were **updated**, which rows could not be imported (**row errors**) and which rows were imported but deserve a look (**row warnings**). Each issue names the spreadsheet row and, where it applies, the field, so Sales can fix the sheet and re-import.
+
+Rows are independent: one bad row doesn't stop the others. Problems with the file as a whole are **file errors**: the import is rejected and nothing is imported. These include a file that can't be read, missing required columns, or more than 1000 contact rows.
+
+An import only ever adds information to existing contacts. It never removes tags and never wipes a phone we already have (ADR-0001).
+
+## User Stories
+
+1. As a sales rep, I want to import a whole CSV file of contacts at once, so that I don't have to add them one by one.
+2. As a sales rep, I want to import a file with the same columns as our CSV export, so that I can export, edit in a spreadsheet and import again.
+3. As a sales rep, I want a report of what the import did, so that I know whether it worked.
+4. As a sales rep, I want to see which emails were created, so that I know which contacts are new.
+5. As a sales rep, I want to see which emails were updated, so that I know which existing contacts were changed.
+6. As a sales rep, I want contacts that the import didn't actually change to be left out of the updated list, so that "updated" means something changed.
+7. As a sales rep, I want the created and updated lists in the same order as my file, so that I can match them against my sheet.
+8. As a sales rep, I want the emails in the report to be normalised, so that they match how contactbook stores and shows them.
+9. As a sales rep, I want each problem to name the row number as my spreadsheet shows it (header = row 1), so that I can jump straight to it.
+10. As a sales rep, I want blank lines to count toward row numbers, so that the numbers still match my spreadsheet when the file has gaps.
+11. As a sales rep, I want a row whose cell spans several lines (a quoted line break) to be numbered by the line it starts on, so that the number points at the start of the row.
+12. As a sales rep, I want each problem to name the field it is about (name, email, phone, tags), so that I know which cell to fix.
+13. As a sales rep, I want every bad field in a row reported at once, so that I can fix a row in one pass instead of re-importing to find the next problem.
+14. As a sales rep, I want the valid rows imported even when other rows are bad, so that one typo doesn't block the whole sheet.
+15. As a sales rep, I want a row with a missing or invalid email to be rejected, so that no contact is created without a usable identity.
+16. As a sales rep, I want a row with an empty or over-long name to be rejected, so that every contact has a proper name.
+17. As a sales rep, I want a row with an invalid tag to be rejected, so that bad tags don't creep into the book.
+18. As a sales rep, I want a row with an unusable phone number to still be imported, with a warning about the phone, so that one bad phone doesn't lose me the lead.
+19. As a sales rep, I want a new contact whose phone is unusable to be created without a phone, so that nothing invalid gets stored.
+20. As a sales rep, I want an existing contact whose phone in the file is unusable to keep its current phone, so that a bad cell never destroys good data.
+21. As a sales rep, I want an empty phone cell to leave an existing contact's phone alone, so that a sheet without phone numbers doesn't wipe the phones we have.
+22. As a sales rep, I want a file without a phone column to leave all phones alone, so that I can import a sheet that only has names and emails.
+23. As a sales rep, I want a valid phone in the file to replace the stored one, so that I can correct phone numbers through an import.
+24. As a sales rep, I want the name in the file to replace the stored name, so that I can correct names through an import.
+25. As a sales rep, I want the tags in the file to be added to the contact's existing tags, so that an import never loses tags someone else applied.
+26. As a sales rep, I want an import never to remove tags, so that I can't accidentally strip tags by importing an older sheet.
+27. As a sales rep, I want several tags in one cell separated by `;`, as in the export, so that the file round-trips.
+28. As a sales rep, I want stray spaces and empty entries in the tags cell (`vip; ;lead;`) to be ignored, so that sloppy formatting doesn't fail a row.
+29. As a sales rep, I want names, emails, phones and tags cleaned up the same way as when contacts are added by hand, so that imported contacts look like every other contact.
+30. As a sales rep, I want the same email appearing twice in my file to be flagged on the later row, so that I notice the conflict instead of having one version silently win.
+31. As a sales rep, I want emails that differ only in case or spacing to count as the same email, so that `Ann@Example.com ` and `ann@example.com` are recognised as duplicates.
+32. As a sales rep, I want the first row with an email to claim it even if that row has an error, so that fixing the first row and re-importing gives a predictable result.
+33. As a sales rep, I want completely blank rows (empty lines, or only empty or whitespace cells) to be skipped silently, so that trailing empty rows from Excel don't clutter the report.
+34. As a sales rep, I want a row with fewer cells than the header to treat the missing cells as empty, so that short rows still import.
+35. As a sales rep, I want a row with more cells than the header to be rejected, so that rows with shifted cells don't import garbage.
+36. As a sales rep, I want columns matched by name regardless of order, case or surrounding spaces, so that `Email`, ` email ` and a reordered sheet all work.
+37. As a sales rep, I want to import a sheet with `first_name` and `last_name` columns instead of `name`, so that files from our address-book export script work directly.
+38. As a sales rep, I want first and last name joined with one space, and an empty half left out, so that "Cher" with no last name becomes "Cher".
+39. As a sales rep, I want a row with both first and last name empty to be rejected as having no name, so that the first/last style follows the same rule as `name`.
+40. As a sales rep, I want the file rejected when it mixes `name` with `first_name`/`last_name`, so that two disagreeing name sources are never guessed between.
+41. As a sales rep, I want the file rejected when it has only one of `first_name`/`last_name`, so that half a name column isn't mistaken for a full one.
+42. As a sales rep, I want the file rejected when it has no email column or no name column, so that I'm told immediately that the sheet has the wrong shape.
+43. As a sales rep, I want the file rejected when a column appears twice, so that it's never ambiguous which cell counts.
+44. As a sales rep, I want columns contactbook doesn't know (e.g. `Company`) to be ignored with one warning, so that extra columns in my sheet don't block the import.
+45. As a sales rep, I want a file with more than 1000 contact rows rejected as a whole, so that I split big files instead of half-loading them.
+46. As a sales rep, I want exactly 1000 contact rows to be accepted, and blank rows not to count toward the limit, so that the limit is predictable.
+47. As a sales rep, I want invalid and duplicate rows to count toward the 1000 limit, so that whether a file is too big doesn't depend on its contents.
+48. As a sales rep, I want nothing imported at all when the file is rejected, so that a rejected file never leaves the book half-changed.
+49. As a sales rep, I want a file saved as Excel "CSV UTF-8" (with a byte-order mark) to import correctly, so that the most common Excel format just works.
+50. As a sales rep, I want a file in another encoding rejected with a message telling me to save as "CSV UTF-8", so that accented names are never silently garbled.
+51. As a sales rep, I want a semicolon-separated file (as Excel writes in many European locales) rejected with a message telling me to export as comma-separated, so that I know exactly what to change.
+52. As a sales rep, I want an empty file, or one without a header, rejected, so that I'm told the file is not what was expected.
+53. As a sales rep, I want a broken CSV (e.g. a quote that is never closed) rejected, so that a formatting accident doesn't swallow half my rows into one cell.
+54. As a sales rep, I want a file with only a header to import cleanly with an empty report, so that an empty template is not treated as a failure.
+55. As a developer of the HTTP layer or a CLI tool, I want to pass the file either as raw bytes from disk or as already-decoded text, so that I don't have to decode it myself.
+56. As a developer of the HTTP layer or a CLI tool, I want file errors raised as a ContactError subclass, so that my existing handler for domain errors turns them into user-facing messages.
+57. As a developer of the HTTP layer or a CLI tool, I want row errors and warnings returned as data rather than raised, so that I can render a full report.
+58. As a developer, I want imported contacts to go through the same validation as every other contact, so that lookups by email keep working.
+
+## Implementation Decisions
+
+- **New importer module** exposing exactly this interface, which other code calls:
+  - `import_contacts(repo: InMemoryContactRepository, data: str | bytes) -> ImportReport`
+  - `ImportReport` dataclass: `created: list[str]`, `updated: list[str]` (normalised emails, file order), `errors: list[RowIssue]`, `warnings: list[RowIssue]`.
+  - `RowIssue` dataclass: `row: int`, `column: str | None`, `message: str`.
+- **New `ImportFileError(ContactError)`** in the errors module, raised for every file error. It is exported from the package alongside the other domain errors.
+- **Two phases:** read and check the whole file first, then apply. File-level checks, including the 1000-row limit, run before the repository is touched, so an `ImportFileError` always leaves the repository unchanged. Once the file passes, row errors only affect their own row.
+- **Decoding:** bytes are decoded as UTF-8, accepting a leading BOM. A leading BOM on `str` input is also dropped. Undecodable bytes raise `ImportFileError` with a hint to save as "CSV UTF-8". There is no fallback encoding.
+- **CSV dialect:** comma-delimited only, standard quoting, parsed strictly. A malformed file, such as an unterminated quote, raises `ImportFileError`. If the header, read as comma-separated, turns out to be a single cell containing `;`, raise `ImportFileError` with a message saying the file looks semicolon-separated and should be exported as comma-separated.
+- **Header:**
+  - The header is line 1 of the file. An empty file, or an empty first line, is a file error (no header).
+  - Column names are matched ignoring case and surrounding whitespace. Order doesn't matter.
+  - The email column is required.
+  - The name is required, either as one `name` column or as *both* `first_name` and `last_name`. Only one of the pair, or `name` together with either half, is a file error.
+  - `phone` and `tags` are optional.
+  - A column named twice (after normalising) is a file error.
+  - An unknown column is ignored and produces one warning with `row=1`, `column` = the header text as written, and a short message saying it was ignored.
+- **Row numbering:** `row` is the line number in the file where the row starts. The header is row 1, and every line counts, blank ones included. A row containing a quoted line break is numbered by its first line, and the next row's number accounts for all the lines it used. Python's csv reader reports the line a record *ends* on, so the start line has to be tracked from the previous record's end.
+- **Blank rows:** a row with no cells, or with only empty or whitespace cells, is skipped silently. It produces no issue and doesn't count toward the limit.
+- **Row limit:** more than 1000 non-blank data rows is a file error. Every non-blank data row counts, whether or not it is valid or a duplicate. Exactly 1000 is accepted.
+- **Ragged rows:** missing trailing cells are treated as empty. A row with more cells than the header is a row error with `column=None`, and the row isn't applied.
+- **Field handling** uses the existing validators so values are normalised exactly as elsewhere. Each field is validated independently, so a row gets one issue per bad field, rather than stopping at the first like `Contact.create` does. `column` is the Contact field the issue is about (`name`, `email`, `phone`, `tags`), whatever the header spelling. `message` is the validator's message without the field name.
+  - **name:** the `name` cell; or `first_name` and `last_name` trimmed, with the non-empty parts joined by one space. An empty or over-long result is a row error.
+  - **email:** a missing or invalid email is a row error.
+  - **tags:** the cell is split on `;`, each piece is trimmed, and empty pieces are dropped. Any invalid tag is a row error.
+  - **phone:** an empty cell means "no phone given". An invalid phone is **never a row error**: it is a row warning with `column="phone"`, and the row continues as if no phone had been given.
+- **Errors versus warnings, stated explicitly:** only name, email and tags problems, duplicates and too many cells make a row a row error. A row error means nothing from that row is applied. A phone problem is always a row warning, never an error. It stays a warning even on a row that is rejected for another reason, so the report shows everything to fix in one pass.
+- **Duplicates within the file:** emails are compared after normalisation. The first row with a given email claims it, even if that row has other errors. Any later row with the same email is a row error (`column="email"`, message naming the earlier row, e.g. "same email as row 3") and isn't applied. A row whose email can't be normalised claims nothing.
+- **Applying a valid row**, following ADR-0001:
+  - **Email not in the repository:** create the contact with the row's name, its phone if valid (otherwise none) and its tags. Its email goes into `created`.
+  - **Email already in the repository:** merge.
+    - The name is replaced by the file's name.
+    - The phone is replaced only if the row has a valid non-empty phone.
+    - Tags become the union of existing and file tags.
+    - If the resulting contact equals the stored one, nothing is written, and the email goes into neither list and produces no warning. Otherwise it is stored and its email goes into `updated`.
+- An email appears in `created` or `updated` at most once, and never in both.
+
+## Testing Decisions
+
+- **One seam: `import_contacts`.** Tests call it with a real `InMemoryContactRepository` and CSV content, then assert on the returned `ImportReport` and on the repository's contents (via `get`/`all`), or on the raised `ImportFileError`. Don't test parsing helpers or internal phases directly.
+- Good tests check external behaviour only: what is in the repository after the import and what the report says. They don't depend on how the file is parsed internally, the exact wording of messages (except where the spec gives one, such as the encoding and semicolon hints, which may be matched loosely), or helper names.
+- Cover both input types: bytes (with and without a UTF-8 BOM, and invalid UTF-8) and str.
+- Cover the round trip: CSV produced by the existing export imports into an empty repository and recreates the same contacts.
+- Cover every file error, with an assertion that the repository is unchanged afterwards (including the 1000/1001 boundary and blank rows not counting).
+- Cover each row-error and warning case, including:
+  - multiple issues in one row;
+  - an invalid phone on a new contact, on an existing contact, and on a row rejected for another reason;
+  - row numbers with blank lines and quoted line breaks;
+  - duplicates, including a first occurrence that is itself invalid.
+- Cover merge semantics against a pre-populated repository:
+  - the name is replaced;
+  - the phone is kept on an empty or invalid cell and on a missing column;
+  - tags are unioned;
+  - an unchanged contact appears in neither list.
+- Prior art: the existing test modules are plain pytest functions that build contacts with `Contact.create` and assert on results; the export test asserts on full CSV strings. Follow the same style in a new importer test module, building CSV input as inline strings.
+
+## Out of Scope
+
+- Any separate Lead concept: imported rows are Contacts (a "lead" is Sales jargon for a Contact).
+- Removing tags or clearing phones through an import (ADR-0001).
+- Encodings other than UTF-8, delimiter auto-detection, and Excel (.xlsx) files.
+- A dry-run or preview mode, and undo of an import.
+- Columns beyond name/email/phone/tags (and first_name/last_name); unknown columns are not stored.
+- Streaming or chunked import of files over the 1000-row limit; users split files instead.
+- HTTP/CLI wiring and rendering of the report; those live in other packages.
+- Persistence adapters other than the in-memory repository.
+
+## Further Notes
+
+- Vocabulary follows GLOSSARY.md: import, row, blank row, created contact, updated contact, row error, row warning, file error, import report.
+- ADR-0001 (import merges into existing contacts and never removes data) governs the update behaviour.
+- One decision was carried over without an explicit answer: a phone problem on a row that is rejected for other reasons is still reported as a warning, not an error and not omitted. This was the recommended answer to the last open question during design; revisit it if the user decides otherwise.
+- The repository has no upsert; the importer chooses between `add` and `update` itself, based on whether the email is already present.
